::CW_U05_L01_Q01::Draft excerpt: The poem says I miss the park. Feedback: Add a sensory detail from the park. Which revision best uses the feedback?{
~A. I miss the park a lot.#Incorrect. This repeats the feeling without sensory detail.
=
~C. Parks are outside.#Incorrect. This is general and not a revision.
~D. The poem is now finished.#Incorrect. This does not revise the draft.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q02::Feedback: Your ending is confusing because I cannot tell why the character leaves. What should the revision goal be?{
~B. Change every character name.#Incorrect. Names are not the issue.
~C. Remove the ending completely without replacing it.#Incorrect. That may create a bigger gap.
~D. Add unrelated description of weather.#Incorrect. Weather does not solve motivation unless connected.
=
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q03::Online feedback flags a sentence as long and confusing. What should the writer do first?{
=
~A. Accept the tool's replacement automatically.#Incorrect. The replacement may damage voice.
~B. Ignore the feedback because it came from a tool.#Incorrect. Tool feedback can identify real clarity problems.
~D. Delete all long sentences in the draft.#Incorrect. Some long sentences are purposeful.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q04::Peer feedback: I like it. What is the main problem with this feedback?{
~A. It is too specific.#Incorrect. It is not specific.
~B. It gives too much evidence.#Incorrect. It gives no evidence.
=
~C. It is a convention correction.#Incorrect. It does not address conventions.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q05::Draft excerpt: The room was scary. Feedback: Show the fear through setting details. Which revision best responds?{
=
~B. The room was very scary and really frightening.#Incorrect. This repeats the feeling without showing it.
~C. I decided to write about a beach instead.#Incorrect. That avoids the feedback.
~D. The room had walls.#Incorrect. This detail is too basic to create fear.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q06::Adult feedback and peer feedback conflict. Adult says the ending needs more clarity; peer says the mystery is interesting. What should the writer do?{
~A. Ignore both comments.#Incorrect. Both may contain useful information.
~C. Remove the ending.#Incorrect. That does not solve the conflict.
~D. Add a random explanation unrelated to the story.#Incorrect. Revision should be purposeful.
=
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q07::Feedback: The dialogue sounds the same for both characters. Which revision goal is best?{
~B. Remove all dialogue.#Incorrect. The issue is not that dialogue exists.
=
~C. Add more setting description only.#Incorrect. Setting does not solve similar voices.
~D. Change the title.#Incorrect. The title is not the issue.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q08::Draft excerpt: I was sad. Feedback: Use an action to show sadness. Which revision best uses the feedback?{
~B. I was sad and sadder.#Incorrect. This repeats the emotion.
~C. Sad means unhappy.#Incorrect. This defines rather than revises.
=
~D. The story has a narrator.#Incorrect. This does not show sadness.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q09::Which feedback is most actionable?{
~A. This is good.#Incorrect. It is too general.
~B. I do not like it.#Incorrect. It gives no revision direction.
~D. Make it different.#Incorrect. This is vague.
=
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q10::A writer receives online feedback to replace a poetic fragment with a complete sentence. The fragment is intentional and creates emphasis. What should the writer do?{
=
~B. Always remove every fragment.#Incorrect. Some fragments are purposeful in creative writing.
~C. Accept every grammar suggestion without thinking.#Incorrect. Tools do not always understand creative effect.
~D. Delete the poem.#Incorrect. That is unnecessary.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q11::Draft excerpt: The character is brave. Feedback: Show bravery through a choice. Which revision best responds?{
~B. She was brave, courageous, and brave.#Incorrect. This tells and repeats.
~C. Bravery is important.#Incorrect. This is general.
=
~D. The dog was brown.#Incorrect. Color alone does not show bravery.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q12::What is the best reason to set aside feedback?{
~B. The feedback is specific and useful.#Incorrect. Specific useful feedback should usually be considered.
=
~C. The feedback identifies a real clarity problem.#Incorrect. Real clarity problems should be addressed.
~D. The feedback comes from a reader.#Incorrect. Reader feedback can be valuable.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q13::Feedback: The poem's strongest image is the cracked cup. Which revision best builds on that feedback?{
=
~A. Remove the cracked cup.#Incorrect. The feedback identifies it as strong.
~C. Add a spaceship.#Incorrect. This is unrelated.
~D. Change the poem to an essay about cups.#Incorrect. That changes the genre unnecessarily.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q14::Why should a writer explain a revision after using feedback?{
~B. To prove all feedback is wrong.#Incorrect. The goal is not to reject feedback.
~C. To avoid revising.#Incorrect. Explanation follows revision.
~D. To fill space only.#Incorrect. It has a craft purpose.
=
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q15::Peer feedback: I was confused when the speaker switched from childhood to the present. What should the writer revise?{
~B. Change every image to a color.#Incorrect. That does not solve time confusion.
~C. Remove all memories.#Incorrect. Memories may be central.
=
~D. Add a new character with no role.#Incorrect. This adds confusion.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q16::Draft excerpt: The scene has no clear conflict. Feedback: Give the character something difficult to choose. Which revision best responds?{
~B. Add a list of furniture.#Incorrect. Furniture does not create conflict by itself.
=
~C. Change all punctuation.#Incorrect. Punctuation does not add conflict.
~D. Remove the character's goal.#Incorrect. That weakens conflict.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q17::Which is the best revision plan format?{
~B. Feedback: none; Goal: none; Revision: random changes.#Incorrect. This is not a plan.
~C. Feedback: good; Goal: good; Revision: good.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~D. Feedback: change everything; Goal: delete all voice.#Incorrect. This is not targeted.
=
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q18::Feedback: The speaker's voice is strong, but one image is confusing. What is the best revision approach?{
=
~B. Rewrite the whole piece in a formal academic voice.#Incorrect. That may erase the strong voice.
~C. Ignore the confusing image.#Incorrect. The feedback identifies a real issue.
~D. Remove all images.#Incorrect. Only one image is the problem.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q19::Draft excerpt: The poem repeats the word cold six times. Feedback: The repetition works at first but becomes distracting. What should the writer do?{
~A. Remove every repeated word automatically.#Incorrect. Some repetition can be purposeful.
=
~B. Keep all repetitions because feedback never matters.#Incorrect. The reader identified a pattern problem.
~D. Add more cold repetitions.#Incorrect. That likely increases the problem.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q20::Which feedback best supports revision for structure?{
~B. The word blue appears twice.#Incorrect. That is word-level, not structure.
~C. I like the main character.#Incorrect. This is not structural feedback.
=
~D. Use a comma in sentence two.#Incorrect. That is convention feedback.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q21::A writer receives feedback from three sources. What should the writer do first?{
=
~B. Accept the longest comment automatically.#Incorrect. Length does not equal usefulness.
~C. Reject all comments.#Incorrect. Some may be useful.
~D. Revise without reading the comments.#Incorrect. Feedback must be considered.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q22::Which revision best protects writer purpose? Purpose: create quiet grief. Feedback: Add more emotion.{
~B. Add five exclamation points.#Incorrect. That may break the quiet mood.
~C. Turn the piece into a comedy.#Incorrect. That changes the purpose.
=
~D. Remove the grief entirely.#Incorrect. That removes the purpose.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q23::Which online feedback should be treated with caution in creative writing?{
~B. A spelling correction for an accidental typo.#Incorrect. This is usually useful.
~C. A warning about a missing period in standard prose.#Incorrect. This may be useful.
~D. A note that a word may be misspelled.#Incorrect. This should be checked.
=
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q24::Feedback: The reader wants to know what the character wants. Which revision best responds?{
~B. Add a random weather detail.#Incorrect. Weather does not explain the goal.
=
~C. Remove the character from the story.#Incorrect. That avoids the problem.
~D. Change the font.#Incorrect. Formatting does not clarify desire.
}

::CW_U05_L01_Q25::Which sentence best explains a revision?{
=
~A. I changed stuff because it was bad.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~C. The draft is better now.#Incorrect. This does not explain how.
~D. Someone told me to revise.#Incorrect. This does not explain the revision.
}
